## Module system

Implement a module registry.

Every module must define:

```js
{
  type: "bio",
  label: "Bio",
  icon: "user",
  defaultData: () => ({ ... }),
  validate(data) {},
  render(data, context) {},
  renderEditor(data, callbacks) {},
  export(data) {},
  import(data) {}
}
```

Requirements:

- Modules must be independently renderable.
- A broken module must not crash the whole profile.
- Unknown module types must render as a recoverable "unsupported module" card.
- Modules must support enabled/disabled state.
- Modules must support ordering.
- Module content must remain structured JSON.
- Add modules through a registry, not conditionals scattered throughout the codebase.
- Preserve unknown module payloads during import/export whenever possible.

Initial modules:

- Profile header.
- Status / now card.
- Bio / about card.
- Link list.
- Top Constellation, the modern equivalent of Top 8.
- Guestbook.
- Badge shelf.
- Image gallery using safe image URLs in the prototype.
- Music card, mute-by-default and user-initiated playback only.
- Web ring / discovery links.
- Devlog / bulletin module.
- Text shrine module for fandoms, projects, original characters, or hobbies.

## Theme system

Use a token-first theme system.

Themes must be data-driven and stored separately from profile data. A starter theme defines defaults, while profile-level tokens override theme defaults.

Theme objects should look like:

```js
{
  id: "y2k-chrome",
  name: "Y2K Chrome",
  description: "Glossy panels, cool blues, reflective accents.",
  tags: ["y2k", "glossy", "retro"],
  previewColors: ["#dbeafe", "#38bdf8", "#1d4ed8"],
  tokens: {
    "--page-bg": "#dbeafe",
    "--page-text": "#0f172a",
    "--panel-bg": "rgba(255,255,255,.84)",
    "--panel-text": "#0f172a",
    "--accent": "#0284c7",
    "--accent-contrast": "#ffffff",
    "--border-color": "#7dd3fc",
    "--font-body": "Arial, sans-serif",
    "--font-display": "\"Trebuchet MS\", sans-serif",
    "--radius-sm": "6px",
    "--radius-md": "16px",
    "--shadow": "0 10px 24px rgba(14, 116, 144, .22)"
  },
  allowedDecorations: {
    cursorTrail: false,
    animatedBackground: false,
    scanlines: false
  }
}
```

Provide at least these starter themes:

- Y2K Chrome.
- Scene / Neon.
- Pixel RPG Tavern.
- Soft Angelcore.
- CRT Terminal.
- Indie Devlog.
- Zine Punk.
- Minimal Reader.

Use CSS custom properties for styling:

```css
.profile-page {
  color: var(--page-text);
  background: var(--page-bg);
  font-family: var(--font-body);
}

.profile-panel {
  color: var(--panel-text);
  background: var(--panel-bg);
  border: 2px solid var(--border-color);
  border-radius: var(--radius-md);
  box-shadow: var(--shadow);
}

.profile-title {
  color: var(--accent);
  font-family: var(--font-display);
}
```

Never hardcode theme colors directly inside component CSS unless they are intentional fallback values.

## Theme editor UX

Support three levels of customization.

### Starter mode

Users select a starter theme and can reset to its defaults.

### Visual editor

Expose simple controls for:

- Page background color or safe background image.
- Panel color and transparency.
- Text and accent colors.
- Border style, width, and color.
- Display and body fonts.
- Panel radius.
- Shadows.
- Background tiling.
- Optional subtle texture.
- Layout density.
- Profile header alignment.
- Module visibility and order.

### Advanced CSS mode

Expose a CSS text area only after a clear warning:

> Custom CSS can change your page's appearance. It cannot run JavaScript, create popups, or access other accounts.

Requirements:

- Custom CSS must apply only to the profile preview container.
- Use a generated per-profile scope class, such as `.profile-scope--voidarcade`.
- Never allow CSS that affects the application shell, editor UI, dialogs, or other profiles.
- Do not insert raw untrusted CSS into the global document stylesheet.
- Use a CSS parser/scoper rather than naïve string replacement in production.
- Reject or remove unsafe or unsupported constructs.
- Limit stylesheet size, selector count, rule count, URL count, and nesting depth.
- Save draft edits separately from published edits.
- Include reset, undo, redo, and "disable custom CSS" controls.
- Provide a safe preview mode that excludes custom CSS.
- Provide CSS lint feedback for invalid values and inaccessible color combinations.

## CSS safety policy

For the MVP, allow only a conservative subset.

Allow:

- Scoped class selectors within the profile container.
- Element selectors only when automatically scoped.
- CSS custom property declarations prefixed with `--user-`.
- Common visual properties: `color`, `background`, `background-color`, `border`, `border-color`, `border-radius`, `box-shadow`, `font-family`, `font-size`, `font-weight`, `font-style`, `letter-spacing`, `line-height`, `margin`, `padding`, `gap`, `text-align`, `opacity`, `transform`, `filter`, `display`, `flex`, `grid`, and animation properties subject to motion preferences.
- `@media` rules for responsive layouts and `prefers-reduced-motion`.

Reject:

- `@import`.
- `@font-face` in the initial prototype.
- `@namespace`.
- `behavior`.
- `expression`.
- `-moz-binding`.
- `url(javascript:...)`.
- Remote CSS imports.
- Attempts to escape the profile scope.
- Rules targeting `html`, `body`, `:root`, `iframe`, `dialog`, `script`, editor selectors, or application navigation.
- Fixed overlays that obscure navigation or safety controls.
- Excessive animation durations or infinite rapid flashing.
- Any CSS that makes critical controls permanently invisible or unreachable.

Do not claim CSS sanitization alone is a complete security boundary. If the product later supports arbitrary HTML/CSS, render it in a dedicated isolated origin and sandboxed iframe with a restrictive Content Security Policy.

## Content safety

Do not support arbitrary user HTML in the MVP.

For text content:

- Render plain text by default.
- If rich text is added, use a narrow allowlist of semantic tags.
- Strip event-handler attributes such as `onclick`.
- Reject scripts, forms, iframes, objects, embeds, SVG scripts, and inline JavaScript URLs.
- Validate URLs before rendering links, images, audio, or embeds.
- Prefer `https:` URLs.
- Use `rel="noopener noreferrer"` on externally opened links.
- Add `target="_blank"` only when appropriate and clearly signaled.

When the project adds rich HTML later, sanitize it with a maintained allowlist-based sanitizer.

## Accessibility requirements

Accessibility is a core feature, not a cleanup step.

Always:

- Use landmark elements: `header`, `nav`, `main`, `aside`, `footer`.
- Use buttons for actions and links for navigation.
- Ensure all editor controls have programmatic labels.
- Maintain visible keyboard focus.
- Support keyboard-only module reordering.
- Announce save, import, export, validation, and reorder results with `aria-live`.
- Make dialogs focus-trapped and dismissible with Escape.
- Ensure color contrast warnings appear in the theme editor.
- Provide "Repair contrast" suggestions that adjust text or panel colors.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`.
- Disable cursor trails, parallax, flash effects, and nonessential animations in reduced-motion mode.
- Make GIF/animation-heavy themes pauseable.
- Provide a persistent "Reader Mode" that disables decorative user theme rules.
- Do not rely on color alone for status, errors, selected states, or required fields.

Example reduced-motion baseline:

```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  *,
  *::before,
  *::after {
    animation-duration: 0.01ms !important;
    animation-iteration-count: 1 !important;
    scroll-behavior: auto !important;
    transition-duration: 0.01ms !important;
  }
}
```

## Responsive requirements

Design mobile-first.

Breakpoints should support:

- Narrow phones: single column, editor panels stacked.
- Tablets: preview and controls can use a split layout.
- Desktop: persistent controls plus live profile preview.
- Wide displays: constrain readable content width while allowing decorative page background.

Do not let custom themes break baseline functionality:

- Navigation remains reachable.
- Reader Mode remains reachable.
- Report/block controls remain reachable in a later social build.
- Text remains readable.
- Content does not create unavoidable horizontal scrolling.
- Interactive elements remain touch-friendly.

## Local-first persistence

Use `localStorage` only for the initial prototype and encapsulate all persistence behind a store API.

Create methods such as:

```js
loadProfile(profileId)
saveProfile(profile)
createProfile(profile)
deleteProfile(profileId)
listProfiles()
saveDraft(profileId, draft)
loadDraft(profileId)
createSnapshot(profileId, label)
listSnapshots(profileId)
restoreSnapshot(profileId, snapshotId)
exportProfile(profileId)
importProfile(jsonText)
```

Requirements:

- Namespace keys, for example `webspaces:v1:profile:<id>`.
- Version all stored data.
- Validate data on read and import.
- Never trust persisted data as valid merely because it came from local storage.
- Provide an explicit "clear local demo data" action.
- Warn users that browser storage is prototype-only and may be cleared.
- Export a complete JSON package containing profile data, theme token overrides, custom CSS, modules, and snapshots.

## Snapshot system

Snapshots are a first-class nostalgic feature.

Users should be able to:

- Create named profile-era snapshots.
- Save date and optional note.
- Preview a snapshot before restoration.
- Restore it without destroying the current state.
- Export a snapshot.
- Browse a visual timeline of profile eras.

Suggested labels:

- "Summer layout."
- "Halloween shrine."
- "First CSS experiment."
- "Version 47: too many GIFs."

Snapshots must be immutable once created.

## Discovery and social concepts

Implement only mock/demo data for social features in the first prototype, but architect for future support.

Future concepts:

- Top Constellation: curated people, projects, games, tools, or inspirations.
- Guestbook: moderated signed messages.
- Web rings: thematic circles connecting profiles.
- Recently redecorated profiles.
- Random profile discovery.
- Browse by visual style, hobby, fandom, medium, game genre, or music taste.
- Friend-of-friend exploration.
- Personal devlogs, shrines, and link pages.

Do not center the architecture around infinite scrolling, engagement ranking, follower-count status competition, or autoplay media.

## Component conventions

Each component should:

- Use a clear custom-element name with a hyphen.
- Receive data through properties or explicit attributes.
- Emit `CustomEvent`s for user actions.
- Avoid directly writing to global state.
- Remain testable outside the full application.
- Have a loading, empty, error, and populated state where applicable.
- Use semantic internal markup.
- Support `part` names for intentional theme hooks.

Example:

```js
class ProfileBio extends HTMLElement {
  set module(value) {
    this._module = value;
    this.render();
  }

  render() {
    const title = this._module?.data?.title ?? "About";
    const content = this._module?.data?.content ?? "";

    this.innerHTML = `
      <section class="profile-panel" part="panel bio-panel" aria-labelledby="bio-title">
        <h2 id="bio-title" part="title">${escapeHtml(title)}</h2>
        <p part="content">${escapeHtml(content)}</p>
      </section>
    `;
  }
}

customElements.define("profile-bio", ProfileBio);
```

Do not use `innerHTML` with unescaped user content. Use DOM APIs or a dedicated escaping/sanitization helper.

## Visual direction

The platform should feel handmade and nostalgic without becoming difficult to use.

Use:

- Playful panels.
- Sticker-like badges.
- Browser-window motifs.
- Optional tiled textures.
- Pixel, chrome, zine, CRT, scrapbook, and dreamy visual language.
- Slight intentional irregularity in theme packs.
- Crisp readable typography and a stable layout underneath the decoration.

Avoid:

- Exact replication of MySpace layouts.
- Forced low contrast.
- Autoplay music.
- Unskippable splash screens.
- Flashing effects.
- Full-page ads.
- Heavy visual noise in default themes.
- Designs that make text unreadable for aesthetic reasons.

## Editor behavior

The editor must have:

- A profile identity panel.
- Theme selector.
- Visual token controls.
- Module manager.
- Add-module menu.
- Drag/reorder controls with keyboard alternatives.
- Live preview.
- Desktop/mobile preview toggles.
- Draft/published state indicator.
- Undo/redo.
- Save and reset controls.
- Snapshot controls.
- Export/import controls.
- Accessibility warnings.
- Reader Mode preview.
- Custom CSS panel behind an "Advanced" disclosure.

Every irreversible action requires confirmation.

Every edit should update the preview without requiring a page reload.

Use debouncing for expensive operations such as CSS parsing, accessibility checks, and storage writes.

## Testing expectations

Write tests for pure logic and security boundaries.

Minimum tests:

- Profile schema validation.
- Migration from old schema versions.
- Module registry lookup and unknown-module fallback.
- Theme token merging.
- Theme reset behavior.
- CSS scoping.
- CSS rejection rules.
- Import/export round trip.
- Snapshot immutability.
- Storage corruption recovery.
- URL validation.
- HTML escaping/sanitization helpers.
- Reduced-motion behavior where testable.

Use fixture profiles containing:

- Long text.
- Emoji.
- Right-to-left text.
- Empty modules.
- Broken image URLs.
- Many badges.
- High-contrast and intentionally low-contrast themes.
- Malformed imported JSON.
- Unknown module types.
- Malicious-looking CSS attempts.

